  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level low is slowly moving through the Northeast this \nweekend with cool, cloudy, and showery weather expected. 850 mb \ntemperatures have fallen to around 0C with soundings showing \nsome destabilization this afternoon where there is clearing. \nRain showers should increase in coverage this afternoon, \nespecially along and west of I-81 where there is currently some \nclearing on satellite. \n\nTonight into tomorrow, cold air continues to slowly filter in \nover the Great Lakes and with flow more northwesterly, some lake\neffect rain showers likely develop. Day time temperatures will \nbe around climatological averages for mid november though cloud \ncover at night will keep lows a little warmer than average. \nRainfall amounts between the showers today and the lake effect \nshowers tomorrow will be mostly light, with less than a tenth of\nan inch expected for most of the area. The Tug hill could get \ncloser to a half inch of rain today with help of topography and \nNEPA may miss out on most of the precipitation tonight into \ntomorrow. \n\nAn additional shortwave dives out of the NW on the back side of\nthe exiting shortwave trough that brings a reinforcing shot of \ncooler air. 850 mb temps drop back to just below 0C allowing for\nmore lake effect rain showers to develop. These will once again\nbe light and limited to the eastern Finger Lakes Region and \ninto CNY. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLate next week into the weekend still has potential for a \nstronger low pressure system to form. Most models and ensembles \ndo have an area of low pressure that forms after several \nshortwaves phase in the central US. The efficiency of the phase \nand timing is still uncertain which will affect both the low \nlocation as well as intensity. The 12Z GFS solution is one of \nthe more bullish runs from the 12Z model suit and has a more \nefficient phase of the shortwaves resulting in a Miller B \nNoreaster. The AIGFS as well as the most recent Euro has a less \nefficient phase and a more suppressed solution keeping the low \nmoving more west to east rather than up the coast into New \nEngland like the GFS. Ensemble member low pressure center \nlocations have shifted a little south with more members \nrepresenting the more suppressed solution. 500 mb trends have \nbeen to a stronger ridge over the Hudson Bay so if that trend \ncontinues then that could mean more models will also trend \ntowards a weaker, suppressed system as well.\n\nPrecipitation type is another area with lots of uncertainty. If\nthe GFS solution is correct and a Miller B Noreaster is able to\nform, the deepening low will help the northwest deformation \nzone of the low to dynamically cool the atmosphere and support a\ntransition to snow. With higher QPF as well snow amounts could \nbe locally large especially at higher elevation with pockets of \n6+ inches possible. A weaker, suppressed solution would struggle\nto get the atmosphere cool enough for widespread snow and if it\ndoes transition to snow, it would be a wet snow that struggles \nto accumulate much. Sounding thermodynamic profiles even in the\nGFS are near freezing through the duration of the heavier\nprecipitation so dynamic cooling is likely going to be very \nimportant in producing a swatch of heavier snow, thus leading\nto a great amount of uncertainty this far out in regards to snow.",
